rview": "First-term Republican Nick LaLota seeks reelection in a Long Island district that leans Republican by about four points. He enters the general election with a decisive financial advantage, having raised nearly $3.8 million and holding over $3.3 million on hand, while Democrat Chris Gallant has raised about $647,000. Expert raters uniformly place the race on the Republican side, with most calling it Likely R and a few Lean R or Solid R.\n\nThe campaign's dominant headlines in mid-September concerned Gallant's confirmation that he modeled bondage gear before entering politics, a story that drew national coverage. A single Democratic-sponsored poll from December showed a close race, but it predates the general election and carries a partisan sponsor. Prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-six chance. The race remains a Republican favorite, with the incumbent well positioned but not fully insulated from a strong national Democratic wave.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1953, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.158, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1553, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.188, "margin": -6.5}]}